# Orchestrator

The root agent acts as **orchestrator** for multi-layer work. Delegate to the
phase-scoped subagents below, store their receipts only, and don't re-read the
files they touched unless verify fails or the user asks.

## Phase folders

```text
.agents/agents/
{{PHASE_FOLDER_TREE}}
```

## When to delegate vs. keep inline

Delegate when the request spans 2+ layers, adds a use case, scaffolds a new
{{DOMAIN_WORD}}, or changes more than ~2 files. Keep inline: a single-file typo
fix, "explain X", a read-only question, or a tiny receipt-driven follow-up on one
already-identified file.

## Plan step

For a **new {{DOMAIN_WORD}}**, a **migration**, or a feature whose shape isn't
obvious, dispatch `plan/{{SCOUT}}` first. It is read-only and reports the
business rules, what already exists, the hexagon role and owner of every target
file, the contracts to fix, and the known gaps in the touched area, as a
structured report followed by its receipt.
For a **small addition to an already-conforming {{DOMAIN_WORD}}**, skip straight
to the touch-area dispatch table in `{{MEMORY_FILE}}` — you already know which
builder owns the file.

**Rules go to `{{DOMAIN_AGENT}}` first.** If the scout lists any business rule,
dispatch `{{DOMAIN_AGENT}}` before anything else in the implement chain. Every
other builder depends on its types, and a rule that has no domain home ends up in
a use case, a state class or a driving adapter.

**Scope gaps explicitly.** When the scout reports gaps in the touched area,
decide per gap: fix now (dispatch the owner), or leave and record. Tell the user
which. A gap whose disposition is `migrate` and whose file this feature touches
defaults to fix now — that is what the disposition means. Never pass a gap to a
builder as "fine for now". That's pre-clearing.

<!-- harness: drop the next paragraph when conformance.migration_doc is empty. -->
For a migration, the plan lives in `{{MIGRATION_DOC}}`. Tell the scout which step
of it this work covers, and update nothing in it yourself.

## Dispatch and parallelism

See [parallel.md](parallel.md) for the dependency graph and the exact rules on
which subagents may share a turn.

Dispatch means an actual Agent tool call with `subagent_type` set to the agent's
name. Reading an agent's `.md` file and then doing its work inline yourself is
**not** dispatch — it skips the isolated context and the receipt boundary the
whole pattern depends on. If you find yourself editing `implement/`- or
`verify/`-owned files directly instead of calling the Agent tool, stop and
dispatch properly.

## Writing dispatch prompts — say what, not how

Give a subagent the goal, the contracts it must fit ({{CONTRACT_EXAMPLES}}), and
the constraints it can't discover on its own. Do **not** specify the internals of
the file it owns. It has its own agent file and project skills for that; you do
not have its context, and a prompt that dictates implementation silently
overrides rules you never read. Its rules outrank your prompt: when they
conflict, it will report `blocked` instead of complying.

**The contract comes from the receipts, never from re-reading files.** Each
implement receipt's `OUTCOME` names what its layer exposes (types and error
kinds, port and fake names, state fields and setters, use-case signature and
input type, presentation return shape, view props and locale keys); a plan
report's "Contracts to fix" names what is agreed up front. Copy those names
verbatim into the next dispatch prompt. If a receipt does not name a contract
the next layer needs, re-dispatch its author to report it — do not open its
files and do not guess.

Concretely, when dispatching:

- **Do** state the interface the neighbouring layers already agreed on.
- **Do** state genuine constraints: which caller mounts it, a known trap in the
  test setup, a naming convention two parallel agents must share.
- **Don't** prescribe internal control flow, local state, or helper structure
  inside the owned file.
- **Don't** pre-resolve a boundary question. If you're unsure which layer owns a
  piece of logic, leave it out and let the agent apply its own rules — or ask the
  user. Writing "X is fine here" makes an architectural decision on behalf of an
  agent better equipped to make it.

### Never pre-clear anything to a verify agent

`{{GATE_AGENT}}` and `{{LAYER_REVIEWER}}` exist to catch what
you and the implement agents missed — including mistakes in your own dispatch
prompts. Telling `{{LAYER_REVIEWER}}` "that deviation is expected and fine"
disables it exactly where it was needed.

Give verify agents scope and context — which files changed, what the feature
does. Never give them verdicts. If a deviation is genuinely deliberate, let the
reviewer flag it and overrule the finding yourself in the open, where the user
can see the decision.

## TDD is enforced by the receipts, not by you

{{TEST_OWNING_AGENTS}} own their own tests and must run them before reporting
`STATUS: done`, red then green. Your job is to refuse to advance the chain on a
receipt that isn't `done` — not to write or fix their tests yourself.

<!-- harness: drop the next paragraph when emit.hooks.receipt_check is false. -->
A `SubagentStop` hook bounces a malformed receipt — or a test-owning builder's
`done` with no red run — back to the subagent automatically. That catches the
shape, not the substance. It is not a substitute for you refusing a receipt that
isn't `done`.

## Scope cap

No implement agent edits more than its file cap per invocation (the caps are
listed in `{{MEMORY_FILE}}`). If a layer needs more, split the work into two dispatches
rather than letting one agent sprawl.

When the planner already split a step to fit a cap, dispatch the split as
planned. Merging two planned steps into one prompt to save a turn breaks the
cap the planner respected, and the gate will reject the dispatch.

## Receipts

See [receipt.md](receipt.md) for the exact output contract every dispatched
subagent must follow: plan agents put a structured report before the receipt,
verify agents may put a findings list there, implement agents end with the
receipt only. Store receipts and plan reports only — never re-read a subagent's edited
files unless a verify agent reports `blocked`/`needs-orchestrator`, or the user
explicitly asks.

## Visibility — surface every delegation and receipt

A subagent's final message is not shown to the user automatically — it only
becomes visible if the orchestrator posts it. Silence after a dispatch does not
mean nothing happened; it means the receipt was swallowed. On every dispatch:

1. **Before dispatching**, post a one-line message naming the agent and its task:
   `→ dispatching {{EXAMPLE_DISPATCH_LINE}}`. For a parallel pair, post one line
   per agent in the same turn.
2. **After the subagent finishes**, post its receipt back to the user — verbatim,
   or as a compact one-liner (`{{EXAMPLE_RECEIPT_LINE}}`) — before the next
   dispatch. Never proceed silently.
3. **If a subagent reports `blocked` or `needs-orchestrator`**, say so explicitly,
   quote the `BLOCKERS` line, and state what happens next (re-dispatch, escalate
   to the user, or stop).

This is also how you and the user confirm the pattern is actually running: the
phase folders and skill descriptions don't call anything by themselves — the only
thing that dispatches `plan/`, `implement/`, `verify/` agents is the
orchestrator's own Agent tool calls, following this skill.

